{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Multimedia Storyboarding and AI Asset Generation","title":"Prompt Logs as Design Evidence","description":"AIL 606 textbook section on curating prompt logs as design evidence: turning a raw AI chat transcript into a curated argument for the final asset, with intent, outcome, and disposition per entry, preserved rejections with reasons, and a verification step.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-01","statement":"Curate a raw AI chat history into a prompt log that argues for the final asset, with each kept entry carrying intent, outcome, and disposition"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish a passive transcript from a curated design log that explains failures, not just successes"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-03","statement":"Preserve at least one informative rejection, with its reason, tied to the learning function the asset must serve"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation","prompting_practice","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"06","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/03/05"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_artifact_signal"],"ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_annotation_signal"],"ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Prompt Logs as Design Evidence\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Prompting Practice, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/03-06-prompt-logs-as-design-evidence.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Prompt Logs as Design Evidence: Prompting Practice, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\nWhen AI tools generate the assets, the visible artifact stops being good evidence of the designer's thinking, because anyone can produce a polished image or paragraph in one click. What distinguishes a designer who made principled choices from one who accepted the first output is no longer in the deliverable; it is in the record of how the deliverable was reached. The prompt log is where that record lives, and it is increasingly the part of a portfolio that demonstrates competence rather than mere access to a tool.\n\nThis section reframes the prompt log from a raw chat transcript into a curated argument for the final asset. A transcript is a passive byproduct; a design log is a deliberate document that shows intent, alternatives considered, suggestions rejected with reasons, and verification of the result. The shift matters because reviewers, instructors, and future-you cannot inspect taste, but they can inspect a log that says \"I tried X, it failed because Y, so I changed to Z and confirmed it against the source.\"\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Curate a raw AI chat history into a prompt log that argues for the final asset, with each kept entry carrying intent, outcome, and disposition.\n- Distinguish a passive transcript from a curated design log that explains failures, not just successes.\n- Preserve at least one informative rejection, with its reason, tied to the learning function the asset must serve.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nTwo students submit the identical AI-generated rock-cycle diagram. The first attaches a one-line log: \"Prompt: make a rock cycle diagram. Kept it.\" The second attaches a three-version log showing a baseline that lacked the transformation arrows, a rejection with the reason (the diagram missed the cyclic process, which is the learning point), a revised prompt that fixed it, and a final verification against the textbook. The deliverable is the same; the demonstrated competence is not.\n\nThe first log proves the student can operate the tool. The second proves the student exercised design judgment, knew what the image had to teach, recognized when an output failed it, and could say why the final version is right. This section asks you to make your log do the second job, because a clean transcript of the winning prompt hides exactly the reasoning a reviewer needs to see.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA design log differs from a transcript on four dimensions, and each is a deliberate editorial act.\n\n| Dimension | Raw transcript | Curated design log |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Intent | implicit; just the prompt text | each kept entry states why it was tried |\n| Alternatives | deleted to look tidy | rejected attempts kept, with the reason |\n| Outcome link | none; output floats free | each prompt tied to what changed and why |\n| Verification | absent | final asset checked against a source |\n\nThe most counterintuitive rule is that **rejections are the most valuable entries**. A kept-and-explained failure (\"v1 had no transformation arrows, which is the learning point, so I rejected it\") shows the judgment that a clean log of only the winner erases. Pruning the failures to look efficient destroys the evidence.\n\nMatch each curated-log dimension to the deliberate editorial act that produces it.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Intent\",\"definition\":\"Each kept entry states why the prompt was tried, citing the learning function it serves\"},{\"term\":\"Alternatives\",\"definition\":\"Rejected attempts are kept with the reason, rather than deleted to look tidy\"},{\"term\":\"Outcome link\",\"definition\":\"Each prompt is tied to what changed in the output and why, instead of floating free\"},{\"term\":\"Verification\",\"definition\":\"The final asset is checked against an authoritative source and the check is recorded\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain why an experienced reviewer treats a kept-and-explained rejection as stronger evidence of competence than a tidy log showing only the winning prompt.\" answer=\"Anyone can produce a polished asset in one click, so the deliverable no longer proves judgment. A rejection that names why an output failed (v1 had no transformation arrows, which is the learning point) shows the designer knew what the asset had to teach, recognized when an output missed it, and could say why. A log of only successes erases exactly that reasoning, leaving the reviewer unable to distinguish principled choice from accepting the first plausible result.\"></self-explain>\n\n\nTwo cautions keep a log honest. **Length is not rigor**: a log of fifteen near-identical prompts demonstrates flailing, not judgment, so curate to the entries that carry a decision. And **verification is non-negotiable** when AI generated content, because the tool is fluent but not truthful, so the log must record where the final asset was checked against the authoritative source. These connect to the course anchors as the locus of intent: a log entry earns its place when its intent cites the learning function the asset must serve (a Mayer principle, a load concern, an access need), so the reader can see the prompt was driven by a learning goal, not by trial and error toward something that merely looked good.\n\n## Worked Example: Turning a Chat History Into a Prompt Log\n\nThe task was to generate a diagram of the rock cycle. Below is the before/after of the prompt log itself.\n\n**Before (raw transcript, only the winner kept):**\n\n> Prompt: \"make a rock cycle diagram.\" Output kept. Everything else deleted to keep it tidy.\n\nProblems: this is a passive transcript trimmed to one line. It shows no intent, no rejected attempts, and no link between any prompt and what changed in the output.\n\n**After (curated evidence with intent, a rejection, and outcomes):**\n\n> v1 \"make a rock cycle diagram.\" Intent: get a baseline. Outcome: looked nice but only three rock types and no arrows showing transformation. Rejected: missing the cyclic process, which is the learning point.\n>\n> v2 \"labeled rock cycle: igneous, sedimentary, metamorphic, with arrows for melting, weathering, heat and pressure.\" Intent: foreground the transformations. Outcome: arrows appeared but weathering and erosion were merged. Kept with a note to verify against the source.\n>\n> v3 (final) added \"show weathering and erosion as separate steps.\" Outcome: matched the textbook process. Verified labels against the source.\n\nAnnotated decision trail:\n\n1. Replaced the transcript with curated entries: each kept prompt states intent and the change it produced.\n2. Preserved a rejected attempt (v1) with the reason, since the rejection carries the design judgment.\n3. Linked every revision to an outcome and to the learning goal (showing the cycle), so length is not mistaken for rigor.\n4. Recorded verification against the content source at the final step.\n\nThe improvement is a log that argues for the final prompt, not a tidy list that hides how the decision was made.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nCurate the log as an argument for the final asset. For each entry you keep, require three fields, intent, outcome, and disposition, and prune anything that carries none of them:\n\n1. **Intent:** Why did you try this prompt? Name the learning function it was meant to serve.\n2. **Outcome:** What did the output actually do, and where did it fall short?\n3. **Disposition:** Kept, rejected, or revised, and the reason. Keep at least one informative rejection.\n\nThen add the two integrity checks: a **verification entry** at the final step (\"checked the labels against the source, corrected one\") and a **curation pass** that removes redundant near-duplicate prompts so length signals decisions, not flailing. A log passes when a reader who never saw your screen can reconstruct why the final asset is the right one.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is \"a complete log means keeping everything.\" Completeness is the enemy here; an unedited transcript buries the three or four decisions that matter under dozens of trivial retries, and the reader cannot find the judgment. The second misreading is treating the log as a record of effort rather than of reasoning. Twenty prompts prove you spent time; they do not prove you made a defensible choice. The valuable log is short, edited, and built around rejections and verification, not a timestamped dump of activity.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A student submits a log of twenty near-identical prompts that mostly tweak colors and fonts. What does this log actually demonstrate?\" explanation=\"Length is not rigor. Twenty cosmetic retries prove time was spent, not that a defensible choice was made; they bury the three or four decisions that matter under flailing. A strong log is curated to the entries that carry a decision, built around informative rejections and a verification step, not a timestamped dump of activity.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Thorough design judgment, because more prompts means more rigor\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Effort and flailing, not judgment, because the decisions are buried under cosmetic retries\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Nothing, because logs are never evidence\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk a real curation. The raw history for a rock-cycle diagram has eight prompts, mostly cosmetic retries about colors and fonts. Curate. Entry 1 (kept as a rejection): \"make a rock cycle diagram. Intent: baseline. Outcome: three rock types but no arrows, so it shows types, not the cyclic process, which is the learning point. Rejected.\" Entry 2 (kept, revised): \"labeled rock cycle with arrows for melting, weathering, heat and pressure. Intent: foreground the transformations. Outcome: arrows appeared but weathering and erosion were merged. Kept with a note to verify.\" Entry 3 (final): \"show weathering and erosion as separate steps. Outcome: matched the textbook process. Verified labels against the source.\" The five color-and-font retries are dropped, with a one-line note that they were cosmetic.\n\nThe curated log is three entries and argues for the final diagram: it names what the asset had to teach (the cycle), preserves the rejection that carries the judgment, links each prompt to a change, and records verification. The walkthrough shows curation is subtraction plus annotation, not transcription.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead any prompt log by hunting for its rejections and its verification step first, because that is where the evidence lives. For each kept entry, check that it states intent, outcome, and disposition; flag entries that are just prompt text with no reasoning. A strong reader can tell a curated argument from a tidied transcript at a glance: the argument explains a failure, the transcript only shows successes. Notice when a long log is actually flailing and when a short log is actually decisive.\n\nA strong reader of design logs can, for any log, point to the most informative rejection, the link between a prompt and the change it produced, and the place where the final asset was verified.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact reaches the stronger standard when the log reads as a curated case for the final asset: every kept entry carries intent, outcome, and disposition; at least one rejection is preserved with its reason tied to the learning function; redundant retries are pruned with a note; and the final step records verification against a source. A reviewer should be able to reconstruct the design decision without the original chat window.\n\nA strong closing note reads: \"I cut five cosmetic retries and kept three entries that carry decisions. I preserved the v1 rejection because its failure, no transformation arrows, is exactly the learning point, which is the judgment a clean log would hide. Each kept prompt is tied to the change it produced, and I verified the final labels against the textbook and corrected one merged step.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the logging discipline from solo asset generation to a team workflow where several designers prompt the same tool against a shared storyboard. The curation rule survives, but ownership and provenance now matter: the log must attribute who tried what, so a rejection's reasoning can be traced to the person who made the call, and a shared verification step prevents two designers from each assuming the other checked the facts. The transfer test is whether you can keep the intent-outcome-disposition structure while adding attribution and a single accountable verification, rather than letting the team log decay into an anonymous merged transcript. If your logging habit only works when one person remembers their own reasoning, it does not scale to the collaborative case where reasoning must be legible to others.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the curated prompt log packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/03-06-prompt-logs-as-design-evidence-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"curated prompt log with intent, outcome, and disposition per entry\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"03.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the curated prompt log.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the curated prompt log for AIL 606 03.06. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links Prompt Logs as Design Evidence, evidence source, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates design learning from simple completion.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nTrace how prompting practice becomes design evidence through evidence-based revision and bounded AI support.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation\",\"prompting_practice\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence","stem":"A learner submits, as a prompt log, the entire unedited chat history with the image generator. Why does this fall short as design evidence?","options":["Chat histories are always too long to grade.","A raw transcript is not curated evidence; it does not show, per kept prompt, what the designer was trying to fix and what the output changed.","The generator's terms forbid sharing transcripts.","Transcripts cannot be stored in the studio."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A prompt log is curated evidence of decisions, not a passive transcript. Each retained prompt should carry the intent behind it and the change it produced.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_6_log_is_just_history","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["prompting_practice"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-01","ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence","stem":"To keep the log clean, a designer deletes every prompt that did not work and keeps only the final winning prompt. What design evidence is lost?","options":["Nothing; only the successful prompt matters.","The reasoning that justified the final choice, including which alternatives were tried and why they were rejected.","The timestamp of the final prompt.","The word count of the log."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Rejected attempts carry the judgment. Keeping at least one failed prompt with a stated reason shows why the final prompt was the defensible choice.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_6_only_winning_prompt_matters","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["prompting_practice"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-03"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence","stem":"A learner argues their design is rigorous because the prompt log is forty entries long. Why is this reasoning weak?","options":["Forty entries is too few to matter.","Log length measures effort, not rigor; the log earns credit only where a prompt change is tied to a learning goal or constraint.","Logs should never exceed ten entries.","Long logs always indicate a faulty tool."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Volume is not quality. A log is rigorous where its entries connect prompt decisions to instructional goals, not where it is simply long.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_6_log_proves_quality","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["prompting_practice"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_6_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_prompt_logs_as_design_evidence","stem":"A log lists prompt version 1 through version 6 with the exact wording of each, but no other notes. What single addition would most strengthen it as design evidence?","options":["The font the prompts were typed in.","For each revision, the observed change in the output and whether it moved the artifact toward the learning goal.","The total time spent prompting.","A screenshot of the prompt box."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A list of prompt versions is only data. Tying each edit to its effect on the output and to the learning function turns the log into evidence of design reasoning.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_6_no_link_to_outcome","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["prompting_practice"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-06-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_6_log_is_just_history","pattern":"prompt_log_as_passive_transcript","description":"Treating a prompt log as a raw chat transcript to keep, rather than as curated evidence of design decisions and why prompts changed","trigger":"learner pastes the full unannotated chat history as the log","feedback":"A prompt log is evidence, not a transcript. For each kept prompt, record what you were trying to fix and what the output changed.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_6_only_winning_prompt_matters","pattern":"discards_rejected_attempts","description":"Logging only the final successful prompt and deleting the failed attempts, hiding the reasoning that justified the final choice","trigger":"learner keeps only the prompt that worked","feedback":"Rejected prompts are part of the evidence. Keep at least one failed attempt and say why it was rejected; that is the design judgment.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_6_log_proves_quality","pattern":"log_existence_mistaken_for_rigor","description":"Assuming that having a long prompt log proves the design is rigorous, regardless of whether the log connects to learning decisions","trigger":"learner points to log length as evidence of effort","feedback":"Length is not rigor. The log earns credit only where a prompt change is tied to a learning goal or constraint, not effort alone.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_6_no_link_to_outcome","pattern":"prompt_change_unlinked_to_outcome","description":"Recording prompt edits without noting how each edit changed the artifact or whether it improved the learning function","trigger":"log lists prompt versions but never states the effect of each change","feedback":"Tie each prompt revision to an observed change in the output and to whether it served the learning goal. Edits without effects are noise.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"51c7e70e417e402d00de566ea09ad5879102d16588306502cf3f3d041f89e359","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}